Z||000005||Indiana_resto_guy||05-29-2001||07:14 PM||||After the initial timing is set and everything is put back into normal mode, the ECM WILL set the advance higher and your numbers are NOT unusual at all.<P>If you would care to share with us the method used to set the timing of the engine as it is now set, perhaps some of us can help.<P>Incidently, turn off the caps, I don't like being yelled at!<P>Food for thought about the crank, the starter is at the rear by the transmission, If the front turns when you start (it runs, right?) the crank isn't broken unless you can hear metal grinding two blocks away. I don't think it would have lasted a full year broken!<P>The engine timing must average 10 degrees before TDC between #1 wire and #4 wire.<BR>Set the engine timing like this (one method of two). Unplug the 4 wire connector at the distributor and start car. Use a timing light on #1 plug wire and set timing to the correct settings (10 degrees Before TDC). Connect light to #4 plug wire to see if it the same. If it is not add the first # from #1 plug and add it to the # from #4 wire and divide by two. The answer you get is what the timing is at. If adjustments are necessary let us know and we may help you out.||||reg||1
